CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a three-dimensional shaded rendering of a curved surface with a flat plane passing through it. The surface is drawn in orange with a graded fill and labeled S near its lower right edge; it arches over like a broad dome whose margins curl downward at the front and the back. A pale blue translucent rectangle cuts through the top of the dome and extends well past it on every side, and the region where plane and surface overlap is tinted a darker olive. A black dot marks the point where the plane meets the crest of the surface, with text above it reading Tangent plane at P0(x0, y0, z0). Two curves are drawn on the surface through that dot, one teal and one dark red, crossing the dome in different directions, and each carries a straight tangent line through the dot that lies flat within the blue rectangle. Text at the lower left reads Curve C passes through P0(x0, y0, z0). Showing tangent lines to two unrelated curves lying in one and the same flat sheet is the whole point of the rendering. The picture defines the tangent plane at a point of a surface as the plane holding the tangent lines to every curve on the surface through that point.

PROJECTION TYPE: perspective
Use the scaffold's true perspective projection after building all geometry. Use this only when the source has real vanishing-point convergence or distance-based size shrinkage; then depth should visibly affect apparent size.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Orange shaded dome surface S (broad arch, front and back margins curling downward, graded orange fill, thin darker orange outline along its cut edges)
  - Italic label 'S' near the lower-right skirt of the dome
  - Pale blue translucent rectangular plane slicing horizontally-tilted through the crest of the dome, extending well past the surface on all four sides, with a light grey/white border
  - Olive-tinted overlap region where the blue plane passes through/behind the orange dome
  - Black dot marking P0 at the crest of the dome where the plane touches
  - Teal curve C1 lying on the dome surface, running roughly left-to-right across the dome through P0
  - Dark red curve C2 lying on the dome surface, running roughly front-to-back across the dome through P0
  - Teal straight tangent line to C1 at P0, lying flat inside the blue plane, extending past the dome on both sides
  - Dark red straight tangent line to C2 at P0, lying flat inside the blue plane, extending past the dome on both sides
  - Text label above the dot: 'Tangent plane at P0(x0, y0, z0)' with leader to the dot
  - Text label at lower left: 'Curve C passes through P0(x0, y0, z0)' pointing at the red curve where it descends the front of the dome

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: surface S is the paraboloid-like dome z = 2.4 - 0.22x^2 - 0.10y^2 over x ∈ [-3.5, 3.5], y ∈ [-3.0, 3.0]; front (+y toward viewer-left) and back edges curl down to roughly z = -0.5, matching the drooping orange skirts in the source. P0 sits slightly right of and behind the true crest at (x0, y0) = (0.6, 0.2), z0 = 2.4 - 0.22(0.36) - 0.10(0.04) = 2.317. Tangent plane: z = z0 - 0.44*x0*(x-x0) - 0.20*y0*(y-y0) = z0 - 0.264(x-0.6) - 0.04(y-0.2) — a very gently tilted, nearly horizontal sheet, drawn as a rectangle spanning x ∈ [-3.8, 3.8], y ∈ [-3.4, 3.4] so it overhangs the dome on every side (in the source it juts out far to the upper-left and lower-right). Teal curve C1: the section of S in the plane y = 0.2, i.e. running left-to-right (screen: from lower-left of the dome up over the crest and out to the right); its tangent at P0 is the line through P0 with direction (1, 0, -0.264). Dark red curve C2: the section of S in the plane x = 0.6, running front-to-back (screen: down the near face of the dome, up over the crest, off the upper right); its tangent at P0 has direction (0, 1, -0.04) — in the source image the red tangent appears steep on screen because it recedes, so keep the true 3D directions and let projection do the work. Both tangent segments extend about 2.6 world units each side of P0 so they poke beyond the dome silhouette, as drawn. Extra swept curve (slider): normal section of S in the vertical plane through P0 with horizontal direction (cos θ, sin θ); its tangent has direction (cos θ, sin θ, -0.44*x0*cos θ - 0.20*y0*sin θ). Colors: dome gradient from #F7C6A0 (upper-left, lit) to #EE8B5F / #E8734A (lower-right skirt), outline #D9663F; plane fill #D6EAF6 at ~0.45 opacity with pale #FFFFFF-grey border; the plane-over-dome overlap reads olive/khaki #B9BE8F (achieved by transparency blending, no separate mesh needed); teal curve and tangent #12796F; dark red curve and tangent #B9322B; P0 dot solid black #000000, radius ~0.07 world units; label text near-black #231F20, serif italic subscripts. Label placement: 'Tangent plane at P0(x0, y0, z0)' two lines, centered above and slightly left of the dot; 'Curve C passes through P0(x0, y0, z0)' three lines at lower-left, beside where the red curve descends the front of the dome; italic 'S' at the lower-right skirt of the orange surface.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "That both tangent lines, although they point in different directions on the surface, lie in one and the same flat sheet — only rotating the view makes their coplanarity unmistakable."
  },
  {
    "id": "selectCurve",
    "type": "click",
    "teaches": "Each curve on the surface through P0 carries its own tangent line, yet every one of those lines sits in the same tangent plane; highlighting one curve/tangent pair while dimming the other separates the two independent paths."
  },
  {
    "id": "planeView",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "View",
    "options": [
      "Surface only",
      "Surface + tangent plane"
    ],
    "default": "Surface + tangent plane",
    "teaches": "The tangent plane is not part of the surface — it is the flat local approximation built from the tangent lines at P0, and fading it in over the bare dome shows exactly what it is spanned by."
  },
  {
    "id": "curveDirection",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Direction of an extra curve through P0 (degrees)",
    "range": [
      0,
      180,
      5
    ],
    "default": 60,
    "teaches": "Sweeping a third curve through P0 in any direction on the surface shows its tangent line always sweeps within the same plane — the defining property of the tangent plane."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "The surface alone",
    "narration": "The orange dome S arches over the domain; the black dot marks P0 on its crest, with a teal curve and a dark red curve drawn on the surface through that point.",
    "state": {
      "planeView": "Surface only"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Tangent lines appear",
    "narration": "Each curve gets its straight tangent line at P0. The two lines head off in quite different directions, but both just graze the dome.",
    "state": {
      "planeView": "Surface only"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "The tangent plane",
    "narration": "The pale blue plane fades in: both tangent lines lie flat inside it, and the plane meets the dome only along the olive contact region near P0.",
    "state": {
      "planeView": "Surface + tangent plane"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Any direction works",
    "narration": "Sweep a third curve through P0 in a new direction — its tangent line stays inside the same plane, which is exactly what makes this plane the tangent plane at P0.",
    "state": {
      "planeView": "Surface + tangent plane",
      "curveDirection": 130
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "azimuthDeg": 40,
  "elevationDeg": 22,
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.68
}

NOTES: Build the dome as a ParametricGeometry / plane grid (80×70 segments) with vertex colors for the orange gradient, MeshBasicMaterial-like flat shading plus a faint lambert term, side: DoubleSide, and a slightly darker back side so the curled-down skirts read. Render the tangent plane as a DoubleSide transparent MeshBasicMaterial (opacity 0.45, depthWrite false) so the dome shows through as olive; add a thin LineSegments border. Draw curves and tangent lines as Line2/fat lines (~3px, 4px when highlighted) with polygonOffset or a small normal-direction lift (+0.01) so surface curves never z-fight with the dome. Click picking: raycast against invisible thicker tube proxies around each curve; selected pair goes to full saturation and 4px, the unselected pair drops to ~0.25 opacity. The plane toggle should animate opacity 0 → 0.45 over ~400 ms and simultaneously fade in the tangent lines. Labels as CSS2DObject HTML anchored to world positions so they stay legible while orbiting; keep leader lines as thin 1px dark grey lines. Clamp orbit polar angle to roughly 5°–80° so the learner cannot fall below the surface.

